From 7b94479bc1261bea8321616a44c72b912699644a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 22:20:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 82/82] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon's bind waits for the acquires
 it was ordered behind
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 5 item 3 (the acquire's wait moves to the GPU)
Why: nvk_queue_submit_bind() is ctx_wait(), ctx_bind(), ctx_signal():
 the semaphores a vkQueueBindSparse waits on must be satisfied before
 its binds take effect, and Vulkan lets an application render into a
 sparse image on the graphics queue and, waiting on that render's
 semaphore, unbind or rebind its pages. Since the acquire's wait moved
 to the GPU, ctx_wait() on the bind context pushes host-engine acquires
 into the bind channel's GPFIFO and returns; that orders work submitted
 to that channel, and the MAP_BUFFER_EX/UNMAP_BUFFER ioctls ctx_bind()
 then issues are not work in a channel — they take effect when they
 return. So the unbind ran while the render it waited for was still
 executing. On a DRM backend the VM_BIND queue is what waits; here the
 calling thread now waits, on the CPU, for the bind context's last
 fence — the fence of the submit that carried the acquires, reached
 only once the host engine has passed them — before the first ioctl.
 A bind with no waits, which is every bind this project's tests issue,
 finds that fence long reached and does not block.
Evidence: nvk_queue.c, nvk_queue_submit_bind():
   result = nvkmd_ctx_wait(queue->bind_ctx, ...);
   result = nvkmd_ctx_bind(queue->bind_ctx, ...);
   result = nvkmd_ctx_signal(queue->bind_ctx, ...);
 nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c, nvkmd_horizon_ctx_flush_gpu_waits(): returns
 VK_SUCCESS as soon as horizon_gpu_submit_waits() has kicked off the
 acquire list; nvkmd_horizon_ctx_bind() comment: "A bind takes effect
 the moment the ioctl returns". nvkmd_horizon_pdev.c: has_sparse is
 true, so nvk_physical_device advertises sparseBinding and the bind
 context is real.
 Class X: cross-built. vk_core/sparse_binding binds only after a CPU
 wait for the fill that precedes it, so it never had a semaphore in
 the way and passes either way; the ordered shape — fill a bound block
 with a signal semaphore, vkQueueBindSparse waiting on it to unbind the
 block, then read the memory back after the bind's fence — is what a
 console has to run to see this: before, the pattern may be missing
 (the write was swallowed after the unbind); after, it must be there.
Upstream: no — the backend is Horizon's.
---
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c  | 36 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
index e00d185..34ad7c3 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
@@ -896,6 +896,42 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_bind(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
    if (flushed != VK_SUCCESS)
       return flushed;
 
+   /* AND THE WAITS THIS BIND WAS ORDERED BEHIND GO BEFORE IT, ON THE
+    * CPU, because there is nothing else to put them on.
+    *
+    * nvk_queue_submit_bind() calls ctx_wait(), then ctx_bind(), then
+    * ctx_signal(): the semaphores a vkQueueBindSparse waits on must be
+    * satisfied before its binds take effect. ctx_wait() above turned
+    * those into host-engine acquires in THIS channel's GPFIFO and
+    * returned — which orders work submitted to this channel, and a
+    * MAP_BUFFER_EX ioctl is not work in a channel. Without this, an
+    * unbind waiting on the semaphore a render into the sparse image
+    * signals ran while that render was still executing, and a bind
+    * over a range the exec queue still reads changed the pages under
+    * it. On a DRM backend the VM_BIND queue is what waits; here the
+    * caller's thread does, on the fence of the submit that carried
+    * the acquires — reached only once the host engine has passed them.
+    *
+    * What it costs: vkQueueBindSparse blocks until its dependencies
+    * resolve, on the bind context alone. The exec channel is not
+    * touched, and a bind with no waits — every one this project's
+    * tests issue — finds no fence newer than the one it last waited
+    * for and returns from the wait at once.
+    */
+   if (ctx->has_fence) {
+      horizon_gpu_result res =
+         horizon_gpu_channel_wait_fence(ctx->chan, ctx->last_fence,
+                                        HORIZON_GPU_NO_TIMEOUT);
+      if (res.status != HORIZON_GPU_OK) {
+         return vk_errorf(log_obj, nvkmd_horizon_result(res),
+                          "horizon_gpu_channel_wait_fence(%" PRIu32 ", %"
+                          PRIu32 ") before a bind failed: status %d, nv "
+                          "0x%08" PRIx32, ctx->last_fence.syncpt_id,
+                          ctx->last_fence.threshold, (int)res.status,
+                          res.nv);
+      }
+   }
+
    /* On a DRM backend a bind goes through the kernel's VM_BIND queue and
     * is ordered against the channel's work. Horizon has no such queue:
     * NVGPU_AS_IOCTL_MAP_BUFFER_EX is synchronous and takes effect when
-- 
